<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarnath</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarnath is a site near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where the Buddha gave his first sermon in the deer park of Isipatana. It became a major monastic centre: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> raised a pillar there whose lion capital is now India's national emblem, and the Dhamek Stupa dates largely from the Gupta period. The monasteries declined after the 12th century, and the site was excavated from the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarnath.jpg" alt="Sarnath" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yusuke Kawasaki · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Buddhist_monasteries_near_Dhamekh_Stupa_Monument_Site,_Sarnath.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sarnath lies eight kilometres northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, near the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and the Varuna. It is also known as the Deer Park, Isipatana and Migadaya. The name comes from Sāranganātha, Lord of the Deer, which refers to a Buddhist legend in which the Buddha, in an earlier life as a deer, offered his life in exchange for that of a doe a king meant to kill; the king was so moved that he made the park a deer sanctuary.</p><p>Here, around 528 BCE according to the tradition, the Buddha gave his first teaching to his five original disciples, Kaundinya, Assaji, Bhaddiya, Vappa and Mahanama, the First Turning of the Wheel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a>. In the Mahaparinibbana Sutta he named Sarnath as one of four places of pilgrimage, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushinagar">Kushinagar</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> flourished at Sarnath from about 600 to 200 BCE, helped by the patronage of kings and wealthy merchants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>. The Chinese monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a>, who travelled in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 400 to 411, saw four large towers and two viharas there. Around 640 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> reported hundreds of small shrines and votive stupas, a vihara about 61 metres high holding a large Buddha statue, and about 1,500 monks of the Sammatiya school. Under the Pala rulers pilgrims and monks came from all over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and the Gahadavala kings, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, also supported the monasteries.</p><p>The site was plundered in 1194, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-aibak">Qutb ud-Din Aibak</a> led his men to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> and Sarnath. Some scholars now dispute that Muslim invasions were the main cause of its destruction, arguing that Brahmanical hostility toward Buddhists was responsible. In 1794 workmen of Jagat Singh dismantled a stupa and its relics were thrown into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>. Alexander Cunningham carried out the first systematic excavations in 1835 and 1836 and identified Sarnath as the site of the first sermon. Friedrich Oertel's excavations of 1904 and 1905 uncovered parts of the Ashokan pillar with its lion capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Dhamek Stupa, 39 metres high and 28 metres across, marks the place of the first teaching. The Ashokan pillar was broken in the 12th century, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lion-capital-of-ashoka">Lion Capital of Ashoka</a>, which is in the Sarnath Archaeological Museum, is the State Emblem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the wheel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> that topped it appears on the Indian flag. The modern Mulagandha Kuty Vihara was opened in 1931 by the Maha Bodhi Society, with frescoes by the Japanese artist Kosetsu Nosu.</p><p>Sarnath is also a pilgrimage site for Jains: the village of Singhpur is believed to be the birthplace of Shreyansanatha, the eleventh tirthankara, and a temple dedicated to him was built in 1824. Several Buddhist countries have built temples and monasteries here.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sarnath” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gupta artistic conventions in sculpture and iconography, seen at <b>Sarnath</b> and in the early cave temples of Udayagiri, were carried across Southeast Asia through trade and religious contact, and shaped the artistic traditions of Cambodia, Thailand, and Java for centuries after the empire itself had dissolved.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Provinces were governed by satraps (kshatrapa) and great satraps (mahakshatrapa), such as Kharapallana and Vanaspara, named on a Buddhist statue dedicated at <b>Sarnath</b> in Kanishka's third year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Ashoka's Lion Capital from <b>Sarnath</b>, carved from polished sandstone over 2,000 years ago, is today the national emblem of the Republic of India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>He gave his first teaching to five former companions in the deer park at <b>Sarnath</b> near Varanasi, an event remembered as the first turning of the wheel of the Dharma.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Govindachandra, the most powerful ruler, fought the Palas and Ghaznavid raiders and patronised learning; his queen Kumaradevi endowed a Buddhist monastery at <b>Sarnath</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/gahadavala-dynasty">Gahadavala dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The Buddha gave his first sermon at nearby <b>Sarnath</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kashi">Kingdom of Kashi</a></li></ul></section>
